+    <section class="journal-section">
+      <div class="journal-header">
+        <h2>Roaster's Journal</h2>
+        <span class="journal-subtitle">Field Notes on Ethical Bean Sourcing</span>
+      </div>
+
+      <article id="journal-article" class="journal-article">
+        <p>
+          Every great cup of coffee begins long before the first crack in our drum roasters—it starts on mist-covered volcanic slopes thousands of miles away. At The Daily Grind, our bean sourcing process is built entirely on direct, multi-year partnerships with smallholder farmers and cooperative washing stations across Ethiopia's Yirgacheffe region, Colombia's Huila highlands, and Guatemala's Huehuetenango valleys. Rather than purchasing anonymous commodity lots through middlemen, our head roasters travel to origin during harvest season to walk the shade-grown plots, inspect soil health, and cup fresh parchment alongside the producers who cultivated it.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          Selective hand-picking is the cornerstone of our quality standard. Because coffee cherries on the same branch ripen at different rates, we pay premiums of 40% to 65% above Fair Trade baselines so pickers can make multiple passes through the groves, harvesting only deep-crimson, peak-brix cherries. Once picked, cherries are floated to remove under-ripe defects and depulped within eight hours. Depending on regional microclimates and water conservation goals, lots undergo either crystal-clear mountain spring washed fermentation for 36 hours or slow, raised-bed African honey and natural sun-drying, where cherries are raked every two hours for three weeks to ensure uniform moisture content of 10.5% to 11.5%.
+        </p>
+        <p>
+          Before any green coffee is sealed in GrainPro hermetic liners for ocean freight, our quality control lab grades every micro-lot for zero primary defects and a minimum Specialty Coffee Association (SCA) cupping score of 86 points. Upon arrival at our roastery, we log full farm-to-cup traceability—including harvest altitude, varietal lineage, and farmer compensation—and design bespoke roast profiles that honor the terroir of each harvest without masking its natural floral, citrus, and stone-fruit aromatics.
+        </p>
+      </article>
+
